Well, let's count it up.

Once I get a result that is not "The cool draught refreshes you" or the
magic fountain effect, I can stop quaffing. I don't regard the minimal
chance of a wish from a water demon as being worth trying for.

Hence at an unknown fountain;

1/7 chance it is magical. In this case I will continue quaffing
indefinitely until it does its thing. A magic fountain never dries up
until it becomes non-magical. So the expected value of an unknown fountain
is 1/7 of the cross-board stat raising.

If I am at a non-magical fountain;

3/10 of them give "cool draught". Of these 1/3 will dry up. Hence there is
a 2/10 chance I will have to quaff again; hence the expected total number
of quaffs is 1 + 1/5 + 1/25 + 1/125 + ...; 5/4 of a quaff. Each bad effect
has a 1/30 chance per quaff; I can expect 1/24 of each bad effect per
non-magic fountain tested. But only 6/7 of fountains are non-magic; hence
each fountain tested carries an average 1/28 of each bad effect.

In other words, each bad effect will crop up once every 4 sets of stat
raises. What are the bad effects?

Of course, some of those 1/30 effects are good. Self-knowledge, see
invisible, the potential wish from a water demon. If you don't have see
invisible, you might be more willing to test fountains.

The bad effects are pool creation drowning pets (not hard to guard
against), water moccasins (not a threat with sufficient combat power),
water demons (ditto, remembering that they are slow enough that any Fast
player may engage them with ranged attacks), a water nymph (there is a
point at which you can handle nymphs), small loss of HP and STR (the
latter obviated by poisres or a unihorn), hunger (big deal at this point
in the game), and the 1/5 chance of cursing each item in main inventory
outright.

The last is the only one that is significant to a mid-game character. Much
of your kit can be left out of the area potentially affected by pool
creation, but it's not going to be wise to strip armour and weapon off and
leave them some distance away. However, I reckon you can get that down to
circa 5 items - in other words, repairing the damage up to blessed armour
will cost you 2 potions of holy water on average.

I'd trade a potion of holy water for 2 cross-board stat raises - any time
you don't dilute a PoGA, you trade a potential potion of holy water for
_one_ cross-board stat raise. Wouldn't you?
